# TODO - The Simpsons Arcade Game

## Objective

Original Konami arcade four-player World set 1;
preserve original four-player cooperative gameplay and pixel art.

## Acceptance

- Use only a selected lawful original edition and locally imported assets.
- Reuse shared engine mechanisms and extend them where necessary.
- Verify observable gameplay or application behaviour, saves and input.
- Report actual Linux, Android and browser acceptance where applicable.
- Run `cargo validate` and record unresolved parity or platform limits.

- Check original input under
  `.reference/games/simpsons-arcade/`.
- If required media is missing, record the blocker in Evidence; recheck
  and clear it when a suitable original edition becomes available.

## Evidence

Local original input found: `simpsons.zip` in the required reference directory.
The archive contains 14 members (7,209,088 decoded bytes). Every decoded
member SHA-1 matches the MAME `simpsons` World four-player set 1 definition
at upstream revision `cdcbdf509292257dcdaedc8d8452f79d89ff20d4`.
Archive SHA-256:

`c88e549ab8f039ec3079b34bc39af6738379d306ca06cf81123f4d3501cf8dfa`

No original binaries were executed. The full MAME download timed out; the
relevant pinned driver metadata was retrieved separately. Local input is
verified; asset conversion, gameplay implementation and parity remain open.
The Rust importer also verified all 14 members with exact configured limits
and reported 7,209,088 decoded bytes without extraction or execution.

The selected source adapter pins this archive as `world-4p-set-1`.
Local read-only graphics conversion succeeded using:

```sh
cargo run --locked -p games-build -- --simpsons-graphics \
  .reference/games/simpsons-arcade/simpsons.zip simpsons-arcade \
  world-4p-set-1 \
  c88e549ab8f039ec3079b34bc39af6738379d306ca06cf81123f4d3501cf8dfa \
  3000000 1048576 7209088 14
```

The converter decoded 32,768 8-by-8 background tiles (2,097,152 bytes)
and 32,768 16-by-16 sprite tiles (8,388,608 bytes) as palette indices.
Synthetic fixtures cover hardware lane ordering and bit permutations.
No original code or pixels were written to tracked storage. Palette
selection, audio, asset publication and observable gameplay remain open.

The same command now builds a validated canonical indexed-tile manifest
with two content-addressed entries. The actual local conversion produced
the expected 10,485,760 payload bytes and deterministic manifest identity:

`d5a9aec63eb8e02d392ba3f954e85c9d908c6499f2d96aecb148f841bcd2cd91`

The read-only command reports identities and discards payload bytes.
The adapter supports private transactional staging, but this command does
not publish a filesystem bundle or render any gameplay.

The read-only command has moved to the host composition crate
`games-build`, preserving the importer/platform dependency boundary.
Using the same verified source and limits, the GNU/Linux host command
`--publish-simpsons-graphics` published the bundle beneath
`.reference/games/simpsons-arcade/bundles/`. Independent
`--check-simpsons-graphics` read both declared payloads and verified
the manifest identity above. Repeating publication returned a collision;
read-back still succeeded, and no staging directory remained.
No assets are tracked. Palette reconstruction, audio, rendering,
reference execution and gameplay remain unverified.

Reference execution is now available locally. The official Fedora 45
MAME 0.289-1.x86_64 RPM and seven dependency RPMs passed signature and
digest checks, then were extracted without installing system packages.
MAME reported the original ROM set good. A network-isolated Bubblewrap
run with a read-only original archive completed 30 emulated seconds.
Two independent fresh-state runs drove four coin, start, direction and
button input ports with 40 press/release transitions. Raw 320-by-224
screen buffers matched by SHA-256 at all six sampled frame checkpoints
(120, 599, 660, 900, 1200 and 1500). A fresh no-input control matched
the first three checkpoints and diverged at the last three. The reference
observations are private and retained under `.reference/tools/mame/`.
They establish repeatable original behaviour and input sensitivity, not
remake gameplay parity. Audio, interpreted scene semantics and portable
save behaviour remain unverified.
